    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d2-a2">

                <pb facs="0108" n="2" sameAs="frus:frus1891/pg2"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >New York</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1890-12-31"><hi rendition="#italic">December 31,
                        1890</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">To <hi rendition="#small-caps">George B.
                        Williams</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">700 Fourteenth Street,
                        Washington</hi>.</p>
                <p>We have just received following cable from Buenos Ayres: “The legislature will
                    probably pass the bill. Am advised by my lawyer that if you resist success is
                    probable, because, according to the constitution, taxes must be equal to taxes
                    of local companies. The latter are untaxed. Please telegraph instructions.” We
                    have replied to employ best counsel to defeat the bill. Can our Government be
                    induced to cable instructions to our minister there at our expense? This is the
                    bill about which I telegraphed and wrote to you on the 20th.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. Stahel</hi>.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
